# Codrops article tone

Reference: [False Earth article](https://tympanus.net/codrops/2026/04/21/false-earth-from-webgl-limits-to-a-webgpu-driven-world/) and `still/tone.md`.

## Voice

- Frank, direct, conversational — like explaining the work to another dev.
- First person for process and experiments: "I found", "I remap", "I tried".
- Personal is fine when understated. Say what you noticed or liked; don't perform emotion.

## Story vs author

- Story sections: narrate what happens ("he stopped", "flowers bloom around him").
- Do not write author intent as story ("I wanted him to stop", "I wrote him to…").

## Avoid

- Fancy or academic phrasing ("shares the same base", "tints mixed on the albedo").
- Sales copy ("what sold it for me", "Not a black CG blob", "I kept coming back to").
- Staccato fragment chains ("Not one trick. A stack." / "Two layers, not one shader.").
- Over-explaining the demo setup ("demo sphere", "I isolate on a sphere") — describe the effect.
- "Cartoon" — use **anime**, **Japanese print**, **ink**, **woodblock**.

## Sentence flow

- Prefer connected sentences over many short ones.
- Use semicolons and subordinate clauses; one thought per paragraph where possible.

## Technical prose

- Name things plainly once, then use shorthand (define N and L before N·L).
- Code blocks for non-obvious work only; skip textbook tricks (e.g. inverted-hull outline).
- Teach core technique first, refinements after (e.g. quantized light → code → noise → outline split).

## Vocabulary for this series

- **Japanese print** / **ink** / **woodblock toon** — aesthetic
- **astronaut** — character in story voice; **character** ok in technical bits
- **Still** — chapter title, italicized

## Examples

**Too performative:**
> I kept coming back to this poster. That shadow is what sold it for me. Not a black CG blob.

**Better:**
> While I was looking at *Akira* for the look of this chapter, I found this poster. The shadow underneath is the part I keep noticing — a cool wash, soft at the edge, like ink on paper.

**Too academic:**
> N·L goes through a soft window, then floor into color levels. Shadow and highlight are tints mixed on the albedo.

**Better:**
> The light part starts with N·L — how much the surface faces the light, where N is the normal and L is the light direction. I remap that value between `thresholdLow` and `thresholdHigh`, then `floor` it into **color levels**.
